---
// A plain-language explanation of Bayes factors and the Cauchy prior, shown above the Bayes charts.
---
<div class="bg">
  <p>
    A p value can only say whether a difference was detected. When it was not, it cannot tell you whether the difference is truly absent or whether the study was too small to see it.
    A <strong>Bayes factor</strong> can. It compares two stories about the same data: there is no difference, or there is a difference of a plausible size.
    A Bayes factor of 10 for no difference (written BF01 = 10) means the data are ten times more likely under the no-difference story.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second story needs a guess about what "a plausible size" means. I used a <strong>Cauchy prior</strong>, the standard default for this kind of test. It is a bell-shaped curve centered on zero with
    long tails: small differences are most likely, and large ones are not ruled out. Its width is set to 0.707 on the scale of Cohen's d. Each chart also shows what happens
    with a narrower and a wider curve, so you can see whether a conclusion depends on that choice.
  </p>
  <table class="scale">
    <thead><tr><th>Bayes factor</th><th>How to describe the evidence</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 to 3</td><td>Anecdotal: barely worth mentioning</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 to 10</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 to 30</td><td>Strong</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30 to 100</td><td>Very strong</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Over 100</td><td>Extreme</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="small">
    The same words apply in both directions: BF01 = 12 is strong evidence for no difference, and BF10 = 12 is strong evidence for a difference.
    Bayes factors near 1 mean the data cannot tell the two stories apart.
  </p>
</div>
